# Caddy in front of bestpricefinder, for HTTPS. Step by step: deploy/local/caddy/README.md.
#
# Phones need HTTPS: browsers allow geolocation only in a secure context, and http://127.0.0.1 counts as
# secure only on the machine itself. Caddy terminates TLS and proxies to the app, which listens on
# 127.0.0.1:8000 only (deploy/local/run-api.sh).
#
# Pick ONE option:
#   (a) LAN, for your own phone on your home network (active below): a certificate from Caddy's internal CA
#       ("tls internal"). Install Caddy's root certificate on the phone once. Set BPF_LAN_ADDRESS to this
#       machine's LAN IP address when you start Caddy.
#   (b) A real domain with an automatic Let's Encrypt certificate: delete the two (a) lines in the global
#       options and the (a) site, uncomment (b), and put your domain (and e-mail) in.
#
# Headers: the app sends its own security headers (Content-Security-Policy with script hashes,
# X-Frame-Options, Referrer-Policy, Permissions-Policy, ...). Caddy passes them through and adds none, so
# nothing is duplicated or overridden. HSTS also comes from the app: set BPF_HSTS=true in the env file only
# with (b). With either option set BPF_TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS=true in the env file, so that the per-IP rate
# limit sees the phone's address instead of Caddy's. Restart deploy/local/run-api.sh after changing them.
#
# Run (from the repository root):
#   BPF_LAN_ADDRESS=192.168.1.20 caddy run --config deploy/local/caddy/Caddyfile.example --adapter caddyfile
# or copy this file to .data/caddy/Caddyfile (git-ignored) and edit the copy.
{
	# (a) only: do not add Caddy's root certificate to this machine's trust stores (here you use
	# http://127.0.0.1:8000 directly), and do not listen on port 80 (no HTTP->HTTPS redirect for :8443).
	skip_install_trust
	auto_https disable_redirects
	# (b) only, optional: the address Let's Encrypt writes to about your certificate.
	# email you@example.org
}

# Shared by both options.
(bpf_app) {
	# Compress text responses (JSON, HTML, JS, CSS). The app has no cookies or secrets, so compression
	# cannot leak any (BREACH).
	encode zstd gzip
	# The app refuses bodies above BPF_API_MAX_BODY_BYTES (64 KiB) itself; Caddy stops bigger ones earlier.
	request_body {
		max_size 64KiB
	}
	# Do not advertise the proxy software (Caddy adds "Server: Caddy" and "Via: 1.1 Caddy").
	header {
		-Server
		-Via
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000
	# No access log: it would record every visitor's IP address. To debug, uncomment this one, which masks
	# the addresses (IPv4 to /16, IPv6 to /32) and keeps 30 days. Use an absolute path.
	# log {
	# 	output file /absolute/path/to/bestpricefinder/.data/logs/caddy-access.log {
	# 		roll_keep_for 720h
	# 	}
	# 	format filter {
	# 		request>remote_ip ip_mask 16 32
	# 		request>client_ip ip_mask 16 32
	# 		request>headers>X-Forwarded-For delete
	# 	}
	# }
}

# (a) LAN: open https://<BPF_LAN_ADDRESS>:8443 on the phone.
{$BPF_LAN_ADDRESS:192.168.1.20}:8443 {
	tls internal
	import bpf_app
}

# (b) A real domain: ports 80 and 443 must reach this machine from the internet. Read the README first:
# this makes the app public.
# bpf.example.org {
# 	import bpf_app
# }
